# How the sandbox's browser is started: one flag per line, comments and blank
# lines skipped. This file is the single home for the tuning — the entrypoint
# starts the resident browser with it and the conformance probe starts its
# throwaway one from the same file, so what the probe measures is what a
# tenant runs. The port, the profile, the cache directory and the log are the
# caller's own and stay with the caller.
#
# The machine this is tuned for has 2-4 GB for everything a tenant does, so
# every flag here is about not spending it. The flag that would spend least of
# all, --single-process, is deliberately absent: one misbehaving page then
# takes the whole browser and its CDP port with it, and the agent loses the
# tool rather than the tab.
#
# The binary behind /usr/local/bin/headless-shell may be Playwright's
# chrome-headless-shell or a full Chromium with anti-detect patches; these
# flags are the intersection both builds accept. --disable-gpu is absent on
# purpose: the anti-detect build paints WebGL through SwiftShader, and that
# flag would turn the disguise off. Headless shell ignores the WebGL flags.

# No display server in the sandbox. The anti-detect Chromium's UA patches
# rewrite HeadlessChrome to Chrome even with this set, so the page does not
# learn it is headless from the product string.
--headless=new

# The container grants no unprivileged user namespaces for Chromium's own
# sandbox, and the isolation boundary is the tenant's machine itself.
--no-sandbox

# A container's /dev/shm is smaller than Chromium assumes; without this,
# renderers crash on pages that are merely large.
--disable-dev-shm-usage

# Software WebGL, the way the anti-detect build disguises the GPU fingerprint
# on a machine that has none. ANGLE + SwiftShader; the ICD path is set by the
# start script when /opt/chrome carries the library. Headless shell accepts
# these and does nothing useful with them.
--enable-webgl
--use-gl=angle
--use-angle=swiftshader
--ignore-gpu-blocklist

# Fewer processes. Site isolation gives every cross-site iframe its own
# renderer — defence in depth against renderer exploits, priced in processes,
# and an ad-heavy page spawns a dozen. Inside a per-tenant machine that
# defence duplicates the boundary the sandbox already is, so it is traded
# back for memory. BackForwardCache keeps whole pages alive for an instant
# back-navigation an agent rarely performs. The renderer cap holds what tab
# count would otherwise spend.
--disable-features=site-per-process,BackForwardCache
--renderer-process-limit=2

# Chromium's own switch for hardware like this: smaller caches and more eager
# GC throughout. The V8 heap cap is per renderer, so a page that will not
# stop allocating kills its own tab rather than the machine.
--enable-low-end-device-mode
--js-flags=--max-old-space-size=256

# Bounded disk cache. The cache directory itself is the caller's, and that is
# load-bearing: left unset it lives inside the profile, and the resident
# browser's profile is on the tenant's mount — a network filesystem where
# this deployment measured 38 ms per small-file create.
--disk-cache-size=104857600

# Idle work for a browser nobody updates or reports on: no component
# updates, no phoning home, no crash uploader.
--disable-background-networking
--disable-component-update
--disable-breakpad

# Headless housekeeping: no first-run state, no audio device, scrollbars out
# of screenshots, and a viewport sized for a page rather than a poster.
--no-first-run
--mute-audio
--hide-scrollbars
--window-size=1280,800
